UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 10: Food safety and hygiene', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people. Common ones include milk, egg, peanut, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, soy, sesame and wheat. A label list is the evidence. “May contain milk” is different from milk in the ingredient list, and neither phrase means the food is safe for every allergy. Cross-contact is traces left by a shared knife or toaster. A student with a milk allergy needs a clean knife, not the one just used for butter.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA biscuit pack says “baked in a factory that handles peanuts” and the ingredients do not list peanut. What can you say, and what can you not promise?\n\nFEEDBACK\nYou can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible. You cannot promise it is safe for someone with a peanut allergy. A parent or the person’s own plan decides.\n\nWATCH FOR\nTasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy. Never test an allergy with food. Reading the label is the task.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nInvent a yoghurt-topped muffin label with three ingredients, one of them milk. Write the sentence a Year 7 student should say to a classmate who avoids milk. Then name one cross-contact risk if the same knife spreads the yoghurt.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Food Technology","learning_intention":"An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people. Common ones include milk, egg, peanut, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, soy, sesame and wheat. A label list is the evidence. “May contain milk” is different from milk in the ingredient list, and neither phrase means the food is safe for every allergy. Cross-contact is traces left by a shared knife or toaster. A student with a milk allergy needs a clean knife, not the one just used for butter.","lesson_number":10,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["FT5-SAF-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Invent a yoghurt-topped muffin label with three ingredients, one of them milk. Write the sentence a Year 7 student should say to a classmate who avoids milk. Then name one cross-contact risk if the same knife spreads the Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using allergen on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy.. Correction: You can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy. Never test an allergy with food. Reading the label is the task. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating allergen on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with allergen, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about allergen with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for allergen. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for allergen. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work","You can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible","Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result"],"question":"A biscuit pack says “baked in a factory that handles peanuts” and the ingredients do not list peanut. What can you say, and what can you not promise?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for allergen; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of label with clear evidence","Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people","You can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named label, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","Name label only, without linking it to allergen","Apply allergen to new material and name the label used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies allergen with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on allergen. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","You can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves allergen","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder","Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent a yoghurt-topped muffin label with three ingredients, one of them milk","Retype the worked example with no new example","Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy","Name label only, with no linked example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies allergen to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about allergen with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating allergen on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using allergen on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for allergen. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with allergen, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy.. Correction: You can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-food-technology-y9-w05-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 alignment. Food Technology 7–10 (2025) says Stage 4 teachers may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (FT4-ADJ-01). This lesson uses FT5-SAF-01 as a pending proposal. The 2019 syllabus remains in teach-out. Not NESA verified. Practical cooking is optional and adult-supervised.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise allergen.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Food Technology","success_criteria":["I can use allergen with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify label and explain why it matters.","I can practise milk independently on a new prompt.","I can notice cross-contact needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder. For a student who must avoid milk, this bar is not suitable, even if the front of the pack says “fruit”. The front claim is advertising. The ingredient list is the check.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA biscuit pack says “baked in a factory that handles peanuts” and the ingredients do not list peanut. What can you say, and what can you not promise?\n\nFEEDBACK\nYou can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible. You cannot promise it is safe for someone with a peanut allergy. A parent or the person’s own plan decides.","explain":"An allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people. Common ones include milk, egg, peanut, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, soy, sesame and wheat. A label list is the evidence. “May contain milk” is different from milk in the ingredient list, and neither phrase means the food is safe for every allergy. Cross-contact is traces left by a shared knife or toaster. A student with a milk allergy needs a clean knife, not the one just used for butter.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy. Never test an allergy with food. Reading the label is the task. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"The ingredient list, not the front slogan, shows the milk.","caption":"Read the list before offering the food.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Front words</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">fruit bar</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ingredient list</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">skim milk powder</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder. For a student who must avoid milk, this bar is not suitable, eve Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder. For a student who must avoid milk, this bar is not suitable, eve Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder. For a student who must avoid milk, this bar is not suitable, even if the front of the pack says “fruit”. The front claim is advertising. The ingredient list is the check. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"A buttered knife can leave milk traces on the next food.","caption":"Cross-contact is a trace, not a visible chunk.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shared knife</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">butter then salad</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Allergy control</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">clean knife</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy. Never test an allergy with food. Reading the label is the task. Here is a more productive model: A muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder. For a student who must avoid milk, this bar is not suitable, even if the front of the pack says “fruit”. The front claim is advertising. The ingredient list is the check.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nYou can say peanut is not an ingredient but the factory warning means traces are possible. You cannot promise it is safe for someone with a peanut allergy. A parent or the person’s own plan decides.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Tasting a tiny bit to “test” someone else’s allergy. Never test an allergy with food. Reading the label is the task. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about allergen?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAn allergen is a food that can trigger a harmful reaction in some people. Common ones include milk, egg, peanut, tree nuts, fish, shellfish, soy, sesame and wheat. A label list is the evidence. “May contain milk” is different from milk in the ingredient list, and neither phrase means the food is safe for every allergy. Cross-contact is traces left by a shared knife or toaster. A student with a milk allergy needs a clean knife, not the one just used for butter. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 10: Food safety and hygiene","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Food safety and hygiene","week":8,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nA muesli bar ingredient list includes skim milk powder. For a student who must avoid milk, this bar is not suitable, even if the front of the pack says “fruit”. The front claim is advertising. The ingredient list is the check.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"The ingredient list, not the front slogan, shows the milk.","caption":"Read the list before offering the food.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 